  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThe forecast area remains on the northwestern periphery of an \nupper-level low which is progged to deepen across the mid- \nAtlantic region over the next couple of days. Various model \nguidance indicates the potential for some spotty showers to \nspread into far north/northwest IL toward daybreak Friday, in \nassociation with an short wave propagating into the upper \nMidwest. This wave will be running into short wave ridging aloft\non the northwestern fringe of the deepening east coast low \nhowever, which along with easterly low-level dry air advection \nis expected to limit the eastward development of precipitation. \nHave maintained some slight chance (<=20%) pops for these \ndecaying showers mainly west of the Fox River Valley across \nnorthern and northwest IL between about 4AM-10AM Friday morning.\n\nBehind Friday's wave, the narrow upper-level ridge is then \nprogged to briefly redevelop into the upper-Midwest Friday night\ninto Saturday before another short wave approaches the upper- \nMidwest Saturday evening. Similar to Friday morning's scenario, \nthis wave will also be fighting the ridge and dry easterly low- \nlevel flow. Some decaying showers will again be possible \nSaturday night across our far western counties of the forecast \narea, but did not include a formal mention in the outgoing\nforecast. Partly to mostly cloudy skies and continued modest \neast-northeast surface winds will likely keep our temperatures \nslightly below average in the mid-60s to lower 70s both Saturday\nand Sunday.\n\nA slower evolution is not unusual in such highly-amplified \nupper air patterns, and the latest global model/ensemble \nsolutions have delayed kicking out the eastern low until early-\nmid next week. As this occurs, there are indications that the \npattern will become more active with an eastward-moving frontal\nzone and surface low developing into the region toward mid- \nweek. NBM initialization depicts this with 40-60% pops later \nTuesday into Wednesday and made no changes at this distance, \nthough there is quite a bit of ensemble spread in the current\nEPS/GEFS/CMCE solutions with the timing/strength and placement \nof features at this time.\n\nRatzer/Borchardt",
